            <ArticleTitle>Clowning as a Complementary Approach for Reducing Iatrogenic Effects in Pediatrics.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>775-782</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">journalofethics.2017.19.8.stas1-1708</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1001/journalofethics.2017.19.8.stas1-1708</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hospitalized children who undergo painful procedures are more susceptible than others to experiencing iatrogenic effects, such as anxiety, pain, and severe stress. Clowns in clinical setting have been found to be effective in reducing children's experiences of these effects during hospitalization and before procedures. This article provides an overview of clowning in health care settings; reviews major studies conducted on clowning for hospitalized children, discussing evidence that clown interventions decrease pain and distress in pediatric patients; and concludes with a discussion of health care clowning as a profession.</AbstractText>
